usage: detective [-h] [--version]
                 {diagnose,converge,decompose,audit,purge,regime,flag,flag-line,parsimony,plan,survey,doctor,extract,censor,receipt,verify-rewrite} ...

Read what a function actually does, pin it with tests, and split it SAFELY —
every rewrite is applied only when a generated suite proves behavior survived.

NEW REPO? START HERE:
    detective regime            # how does this repo import its code and run
                                # its tests — and is anything making every
                                # verdict untrustworthy?
    detective regime --migrate  # fix the part that is Detective's to fix

  Every command below resolves that same regime first and REFUSES on a conflict.
  A number measured against the wrong file is worse than no number: it reads as
  a finding. If a run refuses, `detective regime` is where the reason is.

THEN, read-only:
    detective diagnose path/to/file.py::function   # behavior — one function
    detective plan src/                            # style — AFTER behavior; advisory

positional arguments:
  {diagnose,converge,decompose,audit,purge,regime,flag,flag-line,parsimony,plan,survey,doctor,extract,censor,receipt,verify-rewrite}
    diagnose            the first read for a FUNCTION, once `regime` is clean
                        — what does it actually do, and what to run next
                        (read-only)
    converge            write a complete, minimal pytest suite for a function
                        (the flagship; writes files)
    decompose           split a tangled function into helpers — applied only
                        when PROVEN behavior-preserving
    audit               assess an EXISTING suite: complete? minimal? which
                        tests to prune
    purge               delete regeneratable analysis caches (.detective/ +
                        .wesker/) — never your tests
    regime              read how this repo imports its code and runs its tests
                        — the stage every command runs
    flag                mark a surviving mutant equivalent (default) or a
                        FENCE — an authored must-not
    flag-line           mark an uncovered source line as unreachable (manual
                        oracle — line ledger only)
    parsimony           STATIC repo/module/class SICP map (advisory) — no
                        mutation, no proof
    plan                the first read for STYLE, after behavior — what a
                        codebase's PINNED regions could safely become: priced,
                        gated, every exclusion named (advisory; writes nothing
                        to your project)
    survey              STATIC scan for pure decisions trapped behind an
                        impure boundary (advisory)
    doctor              what is blocking correct USE of this tool — setup /
                        process / taste (advisory)
    extract             propose the pure decision to pull out of an impure
                        shell (advisory) — survey's next step
    censor              propose population-derived CENSORS (forbidden I/O
                        regions) ranked by κ — the §14 corpus loop
    receipt             snapshot a function's specification BEFORE an
                        arbitrary rewrite, for verify-rewrite
    verify-rewrite      prove an arbitrary/model rewrite preserved behaviour,
                        against a receipt

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --version             show program's version number and exit

EXIT CODES (a verdict is also the exit status — CI branches on the code, --json on the field):
  0  clean / success
  1  a real gap or typed REFUSAL — audit --check spec gap; verify-rewrite not PRESERVED; a
     collision/accounting refusal; flag: no such surviving mutant
  2  a conflict / precondition — regime conflict, wrong interpreter, a bad --env, or
     audit --check-strict measurement-incomplete; plan: no such function / nothing to read
  3  INVALID MEASUREMENT, re-run — converge/decompose CUT or stale target; a weak receipt baseline
